(6878455, 1035442, 43463, '<td valign="top">Grats on the great finish, Cory. You need to go back and give this little guy something for his time and trouble to pose for you;-);-) You did a bang up job with the lighting and focus - the textures/details on the snail and the leave are fabulous. The red, blue and green colors are bold and rich in tone that they just pop off the page.</td>', 'Grats on the great finish, Cory. You need to go back and give this little guy something for his time and trouble to pose for you;-);-) You did a bang up job with the lighting and focus - the textures/details on the snail and the leave are fabulous. The red, blue and green colors are bold and rich in tone that they just pop off the page.', '2012-10-08 11:22:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(6878461, 1034422, 134859, '<td valign="top">I liked this image.  <br/>Gave it an 8<br/>One of my top 5</td>', 'I liked this image.  Gave it an 8One of my top 5', '2012-10-08 11:26:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(6878466, 1034422, 10785, '<td valign="top">I looked at this shot several times during the challenge. I was one of your 5 voters. I felt the shot was good overall had good sharpness and color. The composition was very good with excellent foreground, midground and background elements. I think it lacked luminosity. The light is very even out there and the sky is fairly overcast and dull grey. It does have some interest but overall I felt the light of the day was not very good. You represented the scene very well in your shot. So I would say it wasn''t anything you did in particular, just the light at that time did not help the scene. I probably should have bumped you up to a 6 as the processing and camera work was solid overall. Personally I think 5.7xxx is a good score for this and puts it above your average score!</td>', 'I looked at this shot several times during the challenge. I was one of your 5 voters. I felt the shot was good overall had good sharpness and color. The composition was very good with excellent foreground, midground and background elements. I think it lacked luminosity. The light is very even out there and the sky is fairly overcast and dull grey. It does have some interest but overall I felt the light of the day was not very good. You represented the scene very well in your shot. So I would say it wasn''t anything you did in particular, just the light at that time did not help the scene. I probably should have bumped you up to a 6 as the processing and camera work was solid overall. Personally I think 5.7xxx is a good score for this and puts it above your average score!', '2012-10-08 11:32:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(6878467, 1034422, 96664, '<td valign="top">I didn''t vote but I wouldn''t have given it anymore than a 6. <br/> <br/>I think the subject is awesome! But the execution of showing the subject is pretty bland. It seems like the colors are a bit flat, and the depth of field is too short. A lot of the "wow" landscapes like this have EVERYTHING in focus. And I think that is what you might have missed here. <br/> <br/>Like I said though, the subject is awesome... And I think if it were done right, it could have been a top 10 no problem. <br/></td>', 'I didn''t vote but I wouldn''t have given it anymore than a 6.  I think the subject is awesome! But the execution of showing the subject is pretty bland. It seems like the colors are a bit flat, and the depth of field is too short. A lot of the "wow" landscapes like this have EVERYTHING in focus. And I think that is what you might have missed here.  Like I said though, the subject is awesome... And I think if it were done right, it could have been a top 10 no problem. ', '2012-10-08 11:32:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(6878471, 1035438, 43463, '<td valign="top">First off the subject has some strong bones to build on. The vertical composition allows for too much negative space that does not have much interesting elements in the sky to complement the shot. The lens flare acts as more of a distraction rather than complementing the scene as a whole. I would suggest that you take it at a horizontal crop because you can bring your viewer closer to the details of the main subjects of the barn &amp; tree and that that the orientation of the horizontal is a subtle nod to the horizontal lines of the barn. I also suggest that you zoom in to show off more of the details of the barn and the lovely light that is shining through the tree leaves. Bring us in closer by composing it so that you just have a little of the sky above the tree and a little more ground plane just under the shadow of the tree so we can see all the lovely details and textures. Hope this helps.</td>', 'First off the subject has some strong bones to build on. The vertical composition allows for too much negative space that does not have much interesting elements in the sky to complement the shot. The lens flare acts as more of a distraction rather than complementing the scene as a whole. I would suggest that you take it at a horizontal crop because you can bring your viewer closer to the details of the main subjects of the barn & tree and that that the orientation of the horizontal is a subtle nod to the horizontal lines of the barn. I also suggest that you zoom in to show off more of the details of the barn and the lovely light that is shining through the tree leaves. Bring us in closer by composing it so that you just have a little of the sky above the tree and a little more ground plane just under the shadow of the tree so we can see all the lovely details and textures. Hope this helps.', '2012-10-08 11:35:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(6878472, 1034422, 67152, '<td valign="top">A great location - love the waterfall feel of the image.  With regard to the score, I am guessing the long crop cost you some. It seems the more pixels you include in an entry, the larger the score and this image is very pixel starved due to the narrow crop.  The sky seems to be cramped at the top.  <br/> <br/>The white balance also appears to be too cool, with the whites in the water almost appear to have an odd blue tone to them.  I am sure it was not possible, but I wonder if a higher vantage point woudl have helped the image, so we would get a betters sense of the size and shape of the Well. <br/> <br/>Personally, I liked the shot, but I think a ''warmer'' tone and a taller crop would have helped you out quite a bit.</td>', 'A great location - love the waterfall feel of the image.  With regard to the score, I am guessing the long crop cost you some. It seems the more pixels you include in an entry, the larger the score and this image is very pixel starved due to the narrow crop.  The sky seems to be cramped at the top.   The white balance also appears to be too cool, with the whites in the water almost appear to have an odd blue tone to them.  I am sure it was not possible, but I wonder if a higher vantage point woudl have helped the image, so we would get a betters sense of the size and shape of the Well.  Personally, I liked the shot, but I think a ''warmer'' tone and a taller crop would have helped you out quite a bit.', '2012-10-08 11:35:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(6878475, 1034422, 60173, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by mbrutus2009:</b></div><hr/><i> I didn''t vote but I wouldn''t have given it anymore than a 6. <br/> <br/>I think the subject is awesome! But the execution of showing the subject is pretty bland. It seems like the colors are a bit flat, and the depth of field is too short. A lot of the "wow" landscapes like this have EVERYTHING in focus. And I think that is what you might have missed here. <br/> <br/>Like I said though, the subject is awesome... And I think if it were done right, it could have been a top 10 no problem. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>For a single layer shot I don''t think I could have done much better than f/18 for DOF. That shallow DOF comment haunts me no matter what I do.</td>', 'Originally posted by mbrutus2009: I didn''t vote but I wouldn''t have given it anymore than a 6.  I think the subject is awesome! But the execution of showing the subject is pretty bland. It seems like the colors are a bit flat, and the depth of field is too short. A lot of the "wow" landscapes like this have EVERYTHING in focus. And I think that is what you might have missed here.  Like I said though, the subject is awesome... And I think if it were done right, it could have been a top 10 no problem.   For a single layer shot I don''t think I could have done much better than f/18 for DOF. That shallow DOF comment haunts me no matter what I do.', '2012-10-08 11:36:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(6878488, 1034832, 86218, '<td valign="top">Well done Dennis...Always be curious how the other shot would have done compared to this. Still a respectable finish</td>', 'Well done Dennis...Always be curious how the other shot would have done compared to this. Still a respectable finish', '2012-10-08 11:53:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(6878489, 1034422, 89341, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by aliqui:</b></div><hr/><i> For a single layer shot I don''t think I could have done much better than f/18 for DOF. That shallow DOF comment haunts me no matter what I do. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>How did you focus? Did you use <a href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyperfocal_distance" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Hyperfocal distance</a> <br/> <br/>You can use <a href="//dofmaster.com/" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">dofmaster</a> to calculate ... personally I always add on a little just to make sure!<br/> <br/>The light is fairly flat, an hour or so before sunset would make the light come alive more<br/> <br/>cropped in a bit tighter could have helped ... maybe also trying a longer exposure, say 1s, and timing it to leave some longer wave trails a bit like the front of your shot.<br/> <br/>As others have said, white balance looks a little off too, can be changed if shooting in RAW or just by adjusting the colour balance if shot as jpg</td>', 'Originally posted by aliqui: For a single layer shot I don''t think I could have done much better than f/18 for DOF. That shallow DOF comment haunts me no matter what I do.   How did you focus? Did you use Hyperfocal distance  You can use dofmaster to calculate ... personally I always add on a little just to make sure! The light is fairly flat, an hour or so before sunset would make the light come alive more cropped in a bit tighter could have helped ... maybe also trying a longer exposure, say 1s, and timing it to leave some longer wave trails a bit like the front of your shot. As others have said, white balance looks a little off too, can be changed if shooting in RAW or just by adjusting the colour balance if shot as jpg', '2012-10-08 11:53:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(6878490, 1034422, 50641, '<td valign="top">That''s not a bad score for a free study! Didn''t vote but as others have mentioned dpc usually punishes nonstandard dimensions.  Agree that the blue color cast contributes to lack of pop.  Try futzing with channel mixer.  Pump up your contrasting colors, like those orange weeds. And if you decide to do expert editing, stick a seagull on that foreground rock ;-)</td>', 'That''s not a bad score for a free study! Didn''t vote but as others have mentioned dpc usually punishes nonstandard dimensions.  Agree that the blue color cast contributes to lack of pop.  Try futzing with channel mixer.  Pump up your contrasting colors, like those orange weeds. And if you decide to do expert editing, stick a seagull on that foreground rock ;-)', '2012-10-08 11:54:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(6878492, 1035644, 86218, '<td valign="top">Yip..Klassiek is die beste manier om die een te beskryf. Hoop die kleure is besig om groen te word by julle en dat daar goeie reens sal wees</td>', 'Yip..Klassiek is die beste manier om die een te beskryf. Hoop die kleure is besig om groen te word by julle en dat daar goeie reens sal wees', '2012-10-08 11:54:53.000000', NULL, 0),
